---
<p>Anything that reaches outside the program is an effect named in the signature: <code>fs</code> (files), <code>env</code> (environment variables), <code>process</code> (child processes). Public functions declare them with <code>uses</code>; private functions and <code>main</code> infer them; a package can cap which effects it may use (spec §14).</p>

<h2 id="env">Read and set environment variables</h2>
<p><strong>Not in the standard library yet.</strong> The <code>env</code> effect exists ("environment variables and process-wide settings", spec §14), but neither the spec nor the compiler defines a function to read or set a variable, so none is shown. The spec treats startup environment as trusted input, unlike command-line arguments, which are <code>Untrusted</code> (§15.1) and are available as <code>main(ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt; args)</code>.</p>

<h2 id="run">Run a command and capture its output</h2>
<p><code>Io.run(program, args)</code> runs a <code>PATH</code> program with an argument vector, waits, and returns a <code>ProcessResult</code> with <code>exitCode</code>, <code>stdout</code> and <code>stderr</code>. It never uses a shell, so quotes or spaces cannot change the command. A non-zero exit is a result; only a failure to start is an <code>IoError</code>. Arguments are a span: pass a fixed array with <code>.span</code>.</p>
<example name="cookbook-run"/>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">r = subprocess.run(["echo", "-n", "hello"], capture_output=True, text=True)
r.returncode, r.stdout, r.stderr</code></pre>
<note>The example starts <code>echo</code> and <code>sh</code>, so it runs on macOS and Linux. On Windows, <code>Io.runBatch</code> runs a <code>.bat</code> or <code>.cmd</code> file; elsewhere it fails with <code>unsupported</code>.</note>

<h2 id="exit">Exit with a status code</h2>
<p><code>main</code> returning <code>i32</code> makes the value the exit status. The spec defines no call that exits from deep inside; return the code up to <code>main</code>. A <code>main</code> that <code>fails</code> exits with status 1 by default; a variant can carry its own code with <code>@exitCode(n)</code> (§8).</p>
<example name="cookbook-exit"/>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">os.Exit(3)</code></pre>
